10 Terraform Scenarios Every DevOps Engineer Eventually Faces
Most people learn Terraform by creating an EC2 instance.

Real DevOps work starts when the second terraform apply breaks production.

After working with Infrastructure as Code, I noticed the same failures repeat — not because Terraform is bad, but because production systems behave differently from tutorials.

Common situations:
• State file deleted → Terraform recreates infrastructure

• Concurrent apply → state corruption

• Half-failed deployment → tainted resources

• API throttling → rollout stops midway

• Manual console change → invisible drift

• Removing code → unexpected destruction

• Provider upgrade → resource recreation

• Circular dependency → Terraform refuses to run

• Service quota exceeded → deployment blocked

• Backend access lost → infrastructure unmanaged
And the solutions are rarely complex — they’re operational discipline:
✔ Remote state + locking
✔ Drift detection before apply
✔ Version pinning
✔ prevent_destroy for critical resources
✔ Staging validation before upgrades

Big realization:
Infrastructure automation is easy.
Infrastructure reliability is process.

Terraform doesn’t break production — uncontrolled change does.
